<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Liturgy" cic="CCC 1066-1112" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">To be used after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does the word &ldquo;liturgy&rdquo; mean and what does the Catechism say it is?</li>
<li>What are the three Persons of the Trinity and how does each one participate in the liturgy?</li>
<li>What is the &ldquo;total Christ&rdquo; and why is he the subject who celebrates the liturgy?</li>
<li>What is the difference between the common priesthood of the faithful and the ministerial priesthood?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>To Reflect and Write</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no &ldquo;right answers&rdquo; to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Describe the last Mass you attended. What did you feel? What did you like and what was hard for you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="margin-top:8px">Use three adjectives to describe your experience:</p>
<p class="question-block__p">___________ ___________ ___________</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. If you thought that at every Mass heaven opens and the angels and saints are present, would anything change about how you participate?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">What concrete thing would change? (arriving earlier, dressing with more reverence, participating more attentively, preparing beforehand...)</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. What is your favorite liturgical sign or gesture? (holy water, sign of the cross, kneeling, incense, Paschal candle, altar bell...)</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Explain why that sign helps you connect with God.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. What liturgical season are we in? How could you live it in a special way this week?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Look up the liturgical calendar if you don&rsquo;t know. Propose a small concrete action.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. The priest acts <em>in persona Christi</em> (in the person of Christ). Does this help you when you don&rsquo;t like the priest or the homily seems boring?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Why doesn&rsquo;t the liturgy&rsquo;s efficacy depend on how likable or holy the priest is?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Draw Your Church&hellip; with Heaven Open</h4>
<p>Take a blank sheet of paper. At the bottom, draw your parish as it is: the altar, the pews, the people. At the top, draw what Revelation describes: the Lamb, the angels, the saints, the light. And in the middle, draw a staircase or a beam of light that unites the two parts. That is the liturgy: the bridge between heaven and earth. Write underneath: &ldquo;In the earthly liturgy we share in the heavenly liturgy&rdquo; (CCC 1090).</p>

<h4>B. The Colors of the Year</h4>
<p>Draw a circle divided into the seasons of the liturgical year. Color each section with its corresponding color. Next to each section, write one word that defines how you feel during that season:</p>
<ul>
<li>Advent (violet): ___________</li>
<li>Christmastide (white): ___________</li>
<li>Ordinary Time I (green): ___________</li>
<li>Lent (violet): ___________</li>
<li>Eastertide (white): ___________</li>
<li>Ordinary Time II (green): ___________</li>
</ul>

<h4>C. Your Ideal Mass (with Eyes Wide Open)</h4>
<p>In 10 lines, describe what your ideal Mass would be like. Not what you would change about the liturgy (which has a structure that doesn&rsquo;t depend on us), but your inner attitude: how would you enter? what would you offer? how would you listen? how would you receive Communion? how would you leave?</p>
